BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 56 760 Td (Permitpath) Tj T* () Tj T* (Executive Summary: Permitpath) Tj T* () Tj T* (Problem) Tj T* () Tj T* (Small contractors lose a surprising amount of time and money to building permits. A typical) Tj T* (remodeling or trade firm has between two and twenty employees, and the owner usually does the) Tj T* (paperwork at night after a full day on site. Each city has its own application forms, its own) Tj T* (online portal and its own checklist of required attachments, and many of the rules are not) Tj T* (written down anywhere. A contractor who works in three or four cities has to learn three or) Tj T* (four different systems.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The cost of getting it wrong is high. When an application is rejected, it goes back into the) Tj T* (queue, and a correction can add one to four weeks. During that time crews sit idle, materials) Tj T* (are stored, and customers become unhappy. In interviews with 41 contractors over the last year,) Tj T* (34 told us they had lost at least one job start date in the past six months because of a permit) Tj T* (problem. Most said they track their open permits in a notebook, a spreadsheet or their email) Tj T* (inbox, and several admitted they had missed a reviewer's request for changes because the notice) Tj T* (went to a spam folder.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Solution) Tj T* () Tj T* (Permitpath is a desktop application that sits between the contractor and the city systems. The) Tj T* (contractor enters a job once: the address, the scope of work, the licenses and insurance) Tj T* (details, and the drawings. Permitpath then prepares the application for the chosen city,) Tj T* (filling in the fields and flagging anything missing. Before submission it runs the application) Tj T* (against a checklist built from that city's published requirements and from the reasons for) Tj T* (rejection that our users have reported to us. After submission it tracks the status and sends) Tj T* (reminders when a reviewer comments or a deadline is near.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The main difference from other tools is that Permitpath works across cities. Most existing) Tj T* (products are built for one city or are sold to the city itself. We are built for the) Tj T* (contractor, who is the person who actually feels the pain. We chose a desktop application) Tj T* (because many of our users work with large drawing files and prefer to keep their client data on) Tj T* (their own machines, and because it lets us work with several city portals without depending on) Tj T* (any one of them to give us access.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Market) Tj T* () Tj T* (The United States has roughly 700,000 small construction firms with fewer than twenty) Tj T* (employees. We focus first on residential remodelers and the electrical, plumbing and mechanical) Tj T* (trades, which file the most permits relative to their size. If even one in ten of these firms) Tj T* (paid us the price of our standard license, the market would be worth well over 100 million) Tj T* (dollars a year. We do not need all of those firms. Our plan targets the 25 largest metropolitan) Tj T* (areas, where contractors often cross city lines in a single week, and our first goal is 1,500) Tj T* (paying firms.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Business Model) Tj T* () Tj T* (Permitpath is sold as an annual license. Our standard plan costs 780 dollars per year for one) Tj T* (user and up to five cities, and our team plan costs 1,800 dollars per year for up to five users) Tj T* (and unlimited cities. We add new cities as paid expansions of a license only when a customer) Tj T* (asks for one that is not yet supported. Our cost to serve a customer is low: the software runs) Tj T* (on the customer's computer, and our main costs are the engineers who maintain the city) Tj T* (connections and a small support team. We expect gross margin above 80 percent once we reach) Tj T* (scale. We also plan a later add-on for suppliers and insurers, who want reliable data on permit) Tj T* (status, but we are not relying on it in our forecast.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Competition) Tj ET
endstream
