BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 56 760 Td () Tj T* (Our competitors fall into three groups. The first is general project management software for) Tj T* (contractors, which is broad but does not know anything about the rules of a specific city. The) Tj T* (second is the permit portals that cities operate, which are the system of record but are) Tj T* (designed for the city's staff and not for the applicant. The third is permit expediting) Tj T* (services, where a person is hired to file and follow up on applications. These services work) Tj T* (well but cost several hundred dollars per application, which is too much for a small firm with) Tj T* (many jobs. Permitpath offers some of the same benefit at a small fraction of the price. We) Tj T* (think our strongest protection is the growing set of city-specific rules and rejection patterns) Tj T* (that our software learns from its users, which would take a new entrant a long time to rebuild.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Team) Tj T* () Tj T* (Dana Okonkwo-Reyes, chief executive, studies business administration at our university. Before) Tj T* (starting the company she spent three summers working for her uncle's remodeling firm, where she) Tj T* (handled permits and first saw the problem. Marcus Veltrane, chief technology officer, is a) Tj T* (computer science student who built the first prototype and leads our engineering. Priya) Tj T* (Anandakrishnan, head of customer development, studies urban planning and previously interned in) Tj T* (a city permitting office, which gives us a rare view of how reviewers actually read) Tj T* (applications. We are advised by a retired building official and by a small business lawyer who) Tj T* (has helped us with our contracts.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Milestones and Financials) Tj T* () Tj T* (Over the past year we defined the concept, built a working prototype and tested it with 12) Tj T* (pilot contractors in four cities. In the pilot, first-review rejections fell from about 38) Tj T* (percent of applications to about 14 percent, and the average time to approval fell by roughly) Tj T* (nine days. Eight of the twelve pilot firms converted to paid annual licenses, and we now have) Tj T* (23 paying customers and about 19,000 dollars in annual recurring revenue. We have registered) Tj T* (the company and launched a website that has produced most of our recent leads. We have also) Tj T* (raised 60,000 dollars from a small group of angel investors and a regional startup grant, which) Tj T* (we are using for development and for a part-time support hire. Our forecast is 120 customers) Tj T* (and 95,000 dollars in recurring revenue by the end of next year, and 600 customers and about) Tj T* (470,000 dollars by the end of the year after. We expect to reach break-even at about 450) Tj T* (customers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (What We Ask For) Tj T* () Tj T* (We are applying for the competition's prize money and for mentoring. We would use a prize of) Tj T* (25,000 dollars to add support for eight more cities, which is the most common request from our) Tj T* (waiting list, and to hire a second engineer for the summer. Beyond the money, we would value) Tj T* (introductions to contractor associations and to local governments, since both groups influence) Tj T* (which tools small firms trust. We would also welcome advice from experienced founders on) Tj T* (pricing and on when to move from a desktop product to a hybrid product that includes a web) Tj T* (component.) Tj ET
endstream
